            <ArticleTitle>Manipulation under anesthesia for frozen shoulder with and without steroid injection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1188-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study the effect of manipulating a shoulder with adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) under anesthesia with and without corticosteroid injection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomized trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-four patients referred for manipulation of a frozen shoulder.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">The patients were randomized into 2 groups. One group received an injection of corticosteroid and manipulation; the other was only administered manipulation during anesthesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">The degree of shoulder mobility and pain before and after the manipulation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Manipulation under anesthesia increased the mobility of the affected shoulder. Injection with lidocaine and betamethason did not enhance the effect of the manipulation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Manipulation under anesthesia without intraarticular corticosteroids is recommended as the therapy for frozen shoulder.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
